              {
                "text": "Looking at query2's rows, I'm checking whether 5s form isolated cells that get connected into runs of 3s between them - trying to spot where the 0s adjacent to two 5s become 3s.\n\nWait, maybe the 5s trace a connected snake-like path across the grid, and alternating 3,5,3,5... along that path matches all the training values I checked so far.\n\nBut looking at query2, output row0 shows a 3 appearing exactly where the input had a 0 \u2014 that changes my assumption entirely, so I need to re-examine whether the same 0-to-3 substitution happens in the training examples too.\n\nLet me map out the test grid's 5-positions by row and order them left to right by column to see if a similar alternating pattern applies, treating each column position in sequence and alternating 5,3,5,3... across the full span rather than per-cluster. I want to verify this column-based alternation against the training examples before applying it.\n\nChecking column 2 confirms the sandwiched-zero pattern, but column 1 breaks that theory since it has no zero at all. Testing this rule against training data doesn't quite fit either \u2014 one case shows a lone 5 becoming 3 without any sandwich structure, so I'm reconsidering whether the 3 actually marks something about row position instead.\n\nBut that contradicts the earlier column theory since query2's col0 shows 5 staying 5 while col2 becomes 3, not a strict even-column rule. So maybe it's not about column parity but about exactly one 3 marker per selected column, and I need to find which row gets marked in each case, possibly tracing a path pattern like in the training example's column markers.\n\nThat doesn't fit though since training col0 has 5\u21923 while 0 stays, contradicting my earlier reading. Let me reconsider: maybe the rule is that the minority color in each column gets replaced by 3, checking training col0 (0,5,0) where 5 is minority and becomes 3, col2 (0,5,0) same, col4 (0,0,5) minority 5 becomes 3, col6 (0,5,0) minority 5 stays 5 \u2014 inconsistent, so I need to test this pattern more carefully against each column.\n\nFor query2, even columns turn 0\u21923, matching training where even columns turned 5\u21923. Counting colors globally, training had 5s as the minority (11 of 33), and those became 3. Query2 has 5s as majority (8 of 15) while 0s are minority, so the minority color overall seems to be the one converted to 3.\n\nFor the test grid, counting 5s gives 17 of 51, making 5 the minority there too, so I need to convert 5s sitting in even columns to 3 while leaving odd-column 5s and other colors untouched, then work through each row applying that rule.",
